          "question": "Got stung by a wasp an hour ago, my hand has ballooned and now my eyelids look puffy and I feel strange.",
          "answer": "**Get emergency help now. Call your local emergency number (999 in the UK, 911 in the US, 112 in the EU) or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait to see if it settles.**\n\nThe automated check returned \"routine,\" but I don't accept that for your situation. Swelling that spreads beyond the sting site, puffy eyelids, and feeling unwell within an hour of a sting can be early signs of a serious allergic reaction. The emergency guidance in this corpus says to suspect anaphylaxis when symptoms begin within minutes to a couple of hours of an exposure, and lists skin swelling and feeling faint or dizzy among its signs (*Anaphylaxis and severe allergic reaction*, red-flag guidance). Your eyelid swelling and \"feeling strange\" fit that pattern, even though I can't confirm the other signs.\n\nUntil help arrives:\n- Do not drive yourself.\n- Tell the emergency team about the sting and the swelling. If a clinician has already given you an emergency medicine for stings, follow the plan they gave you.\n- Sit or lie down. If you feel faint, lie flat with your legs raised.\n- Get help immediately if you notice any trouble breathing, a tight throat, difficulty swallowing, a hoarse voice, wheezing, dizziness, fainting, or a racing or weak pulse.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
